---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prompt from '../components/Prompt.astro';
import { INSTALL_PROMPT, REPO } from '../data/site';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<Base title="Install Desks" description="One prompt to paste into Claude Code. What it does, step by step, and how to remove it.">
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Open a folder in Claude Code.</strong> A new empty folder, or one you already work in. Desks keeps what is there.</li>
    <li><strong>Paste this.</strong>
      <Prompt id="install" text={INSTALL_PROMPT} />
    </li>
    <li><strong>Start a new session in the same folder</strong> and say, in your own words, something real you need to get done. Claude proposes a short name for the topic and opens it on your yes. If you already know the name, <code>desk my-first-topic</code> does the same.</li>
    <li><strong>When it offers a review key, say yes.</strong> Three minutes, free, and it is what turns a second Claude into several other vendors. To go through every setup question at once instead, type <code>desk setup</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What the prompt does</h2>
  <p>You are telling an AI agent to follow instructions from the internet. Be suspicious of any prompt like that, from anyone, and look first. <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/INSTALL.md`}>INSTALL.md</a> is about 130 lines, and this is all of it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Downloads one fixed version of the repository to a temporary folder.</li>
    <li>Installs into this folder. Into every folder (<code>~/.claude/</code>) only if you ask for that.</li>
    <li>Shows you the list of files it will create or change, and waits for your yes.</li>
    <li>Copies one skill folder and three worker files into <code>.claude/</code> in your folder.</li>
    <li>Adds one clearly marked block to <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, or creates the file with only that block. In a shared project whose <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is not yours to change, the block can go in <code>CLAUDE.local.md</code> instead: Desks reads it from there and leaves the shared file alone.</li>
    <li>Creates an empty <code>desks/</code> folder.</li>
    <li>Runs the review script once, to check that it works and whether a review key is set.</li>
    <li>Reports which files it created or changed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It installs no programs, changes no settings, creates no account, and asks before touching any file that already exists. Apart from that one check, nothing runs, and nothing runs from the network afterwards: the scripts are two plain Python files in the skill folder that you can read.</p>

  <h2>Other ways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>As a plugin</strong>, in a terminal session: <code>/plugin marketplace add rivera-ernesto/desks</code>, then <code>/plugin install desk@desks</code>. This way is less tested than the prompt.</li>
    <li><strong>From a downloaded copy</strong>: download the zip from GitHub, unzip it, and tell Claude where it is in place of the address.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Already have a setup?</h2>
  <p>After installing, type <code>desk adopt</code>. It reads your rules, agents, plugins, connected tools and folders, and proposes how to fit around them. Where your rules and Desks' disagree, yours win. If your folder already has its own way of closing a session, it proposes that as the close, and records it only on your yes (<a href={`${base}how/`}>How it works</a>, step 5).</p>

  <h2>Update and remove</h2>
  <p><strong>Update:</strong> type <code>desk update</code>. It shows what changed and replaces only Desk's own folder.</p>
  <p><strong>Remove:</strong> type <code>desk remove</code>, or delete <code>.claude/skills/desk/</code>, the three <code>desk-*.md</code> files in <code>.claude/agents/</code>, and the marked block in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or <code>CLAUDE.local.md</code>). Your <code>desks/</code> folder is yours.</p>
</Base>
